  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "Owners in North America commonly consider a cluster of **design-forward / distinctive-character lifestyle brands** rather than one single brand family. The names that come up most often in this positioning include **Kimpton, Hotel Indigo, Canopy by Hilton, Moxy, AC Hotels, Andaz, Thompson Hotels, The Standard, Dream Hotels, W, Viceroy, Vignette Collection, Autograph Collection, Tribute Portfolio, Curio Collection, and JdV by Hyatt**.[4][5][10][14][15][18]\n\nA few notes on how these are typically framed:\n\n- **IHG**: **Kimpton** and **Hotel Indigo** are repeatedly described as lifestyle/luxury-lifestyle or distinctive-design brands.[4][10][15]\n- **Hilton**: **Canopy by Hilton**, **Motto by Hilton**, and **Tempo by Hilton** are positioned as lifestyle brands, with Canopy especially associated with local design and neighborhood character.[4][7]\n- **Marriott**: **Moxy**, **AC Hotels**, **Autograph Collection**, **Tribute Portfolio**, and **Design Hotels** are often cited for style-led, lifestyle, or soft-brand appeal.[5][14][15][18]\n- **Hyatt**: **Andaz**, **Thompson Hotels**, **JdV by Hyatt**, and **Dream Hotels** are commonly grouped in lifestyle or distinctive-brand conversations.[15][17][18]\n- **Accor / Ennismore**: **SLS**, **Mondrian**, **Delano**, and **21c Museum Hotels** are explicitly highlighted in North America’s lifestyle growth strategy.[3][8][12]\n- Other often-mentioned owner considerations include **W**, **Viceroy**, **The Standard**, **Bunkhouse Hotels**, and **Me and All Hotels** for stronger design identity or local/cultural differentiation.[6][14][15]\n\nIf useful, I can also turn this into a **brand-by-brand owner shortlist** by segment, such as *upper upscale*, *select service lifestyle*, or *soft brands / collection brands*.",
